## Start here and next implementation step

Start at src/SimpleATH.cpp, not at a disconnected unit test. The native
PCI/RTC/NVM/keycache, EDMA queue, and CCMP/EAPOL packet paths now have
production call sites, but the radio/connection owner is incomplete. In the
current native source, `radioOperational_` and `channelAuthorized_` begin
false and are only cleared on teardown; no successful startup/scan/tune path
sets either true. The existing source-backed
`runAr9565ReceiveOnlyRadioSequence` (INI, PHY mode, board modal, RF bus,
PLL, synthesizer, OFDM timing, calibration) is not called by the native
controller, and is RX-only even when it succeeds. The driver must not
replace missing calibrated/channel/regulatory operations with asserted flags.
An end-to-end integrated, authorized driver is therefore still missing.

Implement one lifecycle owner joining these actual boundaries in order:
PCI provider/gate/D0 -> validated board -> reset/calibrated radio -> mapped
RX/TX/status queues -> IRQ work -> MAC/RSN keys -> native AirPort packets.
For each transition list its call site, owning context, generation, resource
transfer, cancellation and rollback. Add the reverse teardown path before
publishing the service. Test only material transitions with fakes as useful.

Trace an OS packet through encryption/queue completion and a received beacon
or protected packet back to the OS. Trace scan -> selection -> auth -> assoc
-> WPA2 -> controlled data port. Then trace provider loss and sleep/resume,
including key erasure, DMA isolation and a fresh reconnect. Correct buffer
capacity/retry semantics must survive failure between each pair of steps.

Do not flip implementationComplete/verification booleans or preprocessor flags
to manufacture completion. Required normal paths must exist and be reachable;
runtime safety checks remain. Keep this task open until every required callback
has a real source implementation and the Xcode target selects that path.

## Required ownership map to implement

| Resource/state | Producer and consumer | Retirement/invalidation boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| PCI provider and active MMIO capability | Serialized native owner -> NVM/reset/queue clients | Revoke epoch before D0 loss, unmap or close |
| RX DMA packet | Device -> completion owner -> MAC/native packet input | Preserve backing until completion and every consumer releases it |
| Scan/BSS result | Bounded parser/cache -> completed snapshot -> native result copy | Ticket/connection generation; never a borrowed mutable DMA pointer |
| EAPOL and key state | Explicit single supplicant owner -> keycache/data gate | Reject stale connection work; quiesce/remove keys before context release |
| TX OS packet | Native queue -> MAC/security -> device queue | Completion/drop/cancel must resolve ownership exactly once |
| Calibration/channel/power permission | Radio/board/country owner -> every TX path | Invalidate on reset, channel/country change, RF-kill and power loss |

Resolve whether native AirPort/OS or the portable supplicant owns each RSN
step; never let both independently run a handshake or program competing keys.
Keep DHCP/DNS/TCP in the OS networking stack, reached through the real native
packet interface. Do not implement another IP stack inside this kext.

## Required execution-context map to implement

| Entry/context | Permitted work and cancellation contract |
| --- | --- |
| Primary interrupt filter, if selected | Minimal interrupt-safe status/mask/defer operations; no blocking, allocation or command-gate entry. Mapping and callback owner stay retained through delivery drain |
| Work-loop action / command-gated action | Serialize epochs, queue ownership and short state transitions. A command-gate action can run on the caller's thread under the work-loop gate; thread identity alone does not prove serialization |
| Sleepable allocation/NVM/reset worker | Retain operation/provider resources, obey one register owner and a bounded cancellation/deadline contract; return under the gate to revalidate epoch before publishing output |
| Native packet/control callback | Copy or retain inputs according to the verified OS contract; validate connection/scan generation and transfer ownership once. Never retain borrowed request or RX storage past its valid lifetime |
| Stop / sleep / termination | Reject new work and revoke epochs, quiesce/isolate device access, cancel/drain outstanding operations and deferred callbacks, then release mappings/packets/provider. Never wait under a gate needed by the operation being drained |

Bind these rules to actual functions and locks; the table alone is not an
implementation. Account for command-gate reentrancy and every call into an OS
client that could synchronously call back. Do not invoke external callbacks
while holding a private lock unless the verified API explicitly requires it.
Document lock order, sleep eligibility and power acknowledgements for the
selected Tahoe family rather than inferring them from portable fake tests.

Primary source: [IOCommandGate semantics](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/iokit/IOKit/IOCommandGate.h),
[interrupt-filter context](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/iokit/IOKit/IOFilterInterruptEventSource.h),
and [IOService lifecycle](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/iokit/IOKit/IOService.h).

